# Caddy in front of gunicorn. web-and-households ticket 09; the domain itself is
# ticket 13's step 2.
#
# **A household fills in one variable and gets a certificate.** `LEGION_DOMAIN`
# and `LEGION_ACME_EMAIL` come from `deploy/.env` through compose's
# `env_file:` on the `caddy` service - Caddy substitutes `{$NAME}` from its OWN
# process environment when it parses this file, so the variables have to be in
# the container, not just in the shell that ran `docker compose up`. That
# `env_file:` line is what makes this file work; without it both substitutions
# are empty and the stack silently serves plain HTTP on :80 forever.
#
# Every claim below was checked against `caddy validate`/`caddy adapt` with
# caddy v2.11.4 on 2026-09-08, with the variables set and unset, rather than
# read off the documentation.
{
	# The ACME contact address. Let's Encrypt uses it for expiry and policy
	# notices; certificates issue without it.
	#
	# **The quotes are load-bearing.** Caddy substitutes `{$NAME}` textually
	# before parsing, so with the variable unset `email {$LEGION_ACME_EMAIL}`
	# becomes the bare word `email` and Caddy refuses to start:
	#   Error: adapting config using caddyfile: parsing caddyfile tokens for
	#   'email': wrong argument count or unexpected line ending after 'email'
	# Quoted, an unset variable is a valid empty argument and the config
	# adapts cleanly - measured both ways.
	#
	# Setting it also buys a fallback issuer: with an address, Caddy configures
	# Let's Encrypt AND ZeroSSL; with none, only Let's Encrypt. Worth having on
	# a box nobody is watching.
	email "{$LEGION_ACME_EMAIL}"
}

# The site address, and it is doing two jobs.
#
#   LEGION_DOMAIN set (e.g. legion.example.com)
#       Caddy issues and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate for that name and
#       adds an automatic HTTP->HTTPS redirect. This is the VM install. It
#       needs 80 and 443 reachable from the internet under that name, which is
#       why ticket 13's runbook opens both in the OCI security list AND in
#       Ubuntu's own iptables, and why Cloudflare stays grey-cloud so the
#       HTTP-01 challenge reaches this box rather than Cloudflare's edge.
#
#   LEGION_DOMAIN unset or empty
#       `{$NAME:default}` falls back to the text `:80`, so Caddy listens on
#       port 80 for any hostname and applies no TLS - `adapt` emits exactly
#       {"listen":[":80"], ... reverse_proxy web:8000} and logs "server is
#       listening only on the HTTP port, so no automatic HTTPS will be
#       applied". That is the LAN / bare-IP install, and it is the behaviour
#       this file had before the variable existed, so a household that never
#       sets it loses nothing.
#
# The equivalent written out, for anyone who would rather not read a default
# out of a substitution:
#
#     :80 {
#         reverse_proxy web:8000
#     }
#
# **Caddy does not serve the static files itself, on purpose** (ticket 09):
# WhiteNoise inside gunicorn does, because one place that knows the
# `staticfiles.json` manifest is enough, and a second one would be a second
# thing to keep in step with a content hash.
{$LEGION_DOMAIN::80} {
	reverse_proxy web:8000
}
